  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Brexit on UK terms with no ECJ/ECHR control; zero net migration via points-based system, no amnesty, deport foreign criminals, disband sharia councils, ban face coverings, “one law for all”; major law-and-order push (more police/prison officers, tougher prisons, restore Stop & Search, segregate extremists); scrap the European Arrest Warrant and the Human Rights Act in favor of a UK Bill of Rights; a National (not international) Health Service and curbs on ineligible use; cut foreign aid and close DFID (“Trade not Aid”); assert national sovereignty and symbols (200‑mile fishing zone, blue passport); prioritize national energy security over green targets (repeal Climate Change Act, leave Paris/ETS, promote shale). TEXT_D: Keep freedom of movement and seek single market/customs union; unilaterally guarantee EU citizens’ rights and expand refugee resettlement; defend the Human Rights Act/ECHR and curb mass surveillance; advance LGBT+/trans reforms and broader equality agenda; decriminalise drug possession and legalise regulated cannabis; ambitious green program (60% renewables by 2030, oppose fracking, diesel phase-out, zero‑carbon standards); internationalist stance including maintaining 0.7% aid and tighter arms export controls.",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_C consistently argues from TAN commitments—national sovereignty, restrictive immigration, law-and-order, and prioritising national identity—while TEXT_D argues from GAL commitments—civil liberties, open borders/freedom of movement, multicultural equality, and strong environmentalism. Given the electorate profile leans TAN (0.60 TAN vs 0.40 GAL), TEXT_C aligns more closely with these priorities.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_C"
}
